  <result aggregation-status="1" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Annual Output Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator ascending="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output Indicator</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.Number of projects (at the national and local level) integrated anti-corruption solutions in service delivery systems.
2.Number of countries that implemented social accountability initiatives.
3.Numbers of countries that mainstreamed UNCAC and anti-corruption in national development strategies and programmes 
4.Number of anti-corruption institutions with systems and measures in place to prevent corruption.
5.Number of advocacy events that contributed to increased awareness among stakeholders about negative impact of corruption.
6. Number of project-produced knowledge products that have been positively validated through end-user surveys</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline year="2016">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Twenty-seven countries supported in phase 1 (2012-2016) to integrate anti-corruption solutions in service delivery systems (such as in health, education, water and infrastructure, justice and security) to mitigate corruption risks. Baseline Year: 2016
2. Ten countries implemented social accountability initiatives in phase 1. Baseline Year: 2016
3. Five countries trained to mainstream anti-corruption in national development programmes in phase 1. Baseline Year 2016
4. Eighteen anti-corruption institutions supported in phase 1. Baseline Year: 2016 
5. At least 40 countries supported globally per year to promote advocacy campaigns against corruption. Baseline Year: 2016
6 Twenty six knowledge products produced and validated during phase 1. Baseline Year: 2016</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2021-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Up to 3 countries in Africa and Asia-Pacific regions start the implementation of projects aimed at promoting digital governance for transparency, accountability, and anti-corruption. 
2. At least 200 UNDP staff, donors, and programme partners are trained on anti-corruption in the context of 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development and public procurement.
3. Up to 2 countries in Africa and Asia-Pacific regions start the implementation of projects aimed at strengthening national anti-corruption capacities. 
4. At least two knowledge products are developed and widely disseminated and accessible at the global, regional, and country levels. 
5. Up to 20 events are organized on global, regional, and national levels to promote exchange, mutual learning, South-South, and Triangular Cooperation on anti-corruption</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. 7 countries in Africa and Asia-Pacific regions (Bangladesh, Liberia, Nepal, Nigeria, Sri Lanka, Tanzania, Uganda) were selected through a competitive process and started the implementation of projects aimed at promoting digital governance for transparency, accountability, and anti-corruption. 
2. 3,261 UNDP staff, donors, programme partners, and practitioners from the government were trained on anti-corruption in the context of 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development, public procurement, service delivery, technology, and innovation
3. 2 countries in Africa and Asia-Pacific regions (Liberia and Tanzania) start the implementation of projects aimed at strengthening national anti-corruption capacities. 
4. 3 knowledge products exploring linkages between 1) corruption and contemporary forms of slavery; 2) technology and innovation; 3) Anti-Corruption initiative assessment were developed, disseminated, and made publicly accessible at the global, regional, and country levels. 
5. 45 events on anti-corruption were organized/co-organized by ACPIS at the global, regional, and country levels (including 7 special events at COSP9) to promote mutual learning, South-South, and Triangular Cooperation, reaching 3,261 people (of whom 53% are women)</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2020-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Up to 5 countries in the Asia-Pacific region have integrated anti-corruption solutions in service delivery. 
2. Up to 5 countries in Asia-Pacific region have implemented social accountability initiatives. 
3. At least 4 countries trained or received policy and advisory support on integrating anti-corruption into national development strategies and programmes.
4. 100 practitioners from governments and donor partners are targeted to be trained on integrating anti-corruption in the 2030 Agenda.
5. Up to 2 anti-corruption institutions have in place systems and measures to prevent corruption.
6. At least 5 additional countries will be supported in Asia-Pacific region to strengthen anti-corruption efforts. 
7. At least 20% increase in subscription to the anti-corruption portal 
8. Up to 2 countries (1 in Asia-Pacific and 1 in Africa) selected to implement pilot country level projects on strengthening national capacities to integrate anti-corruption in the SDGs.
9. One global and one regional dialogue on anti-corruption organized.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Two (2) countries in the Asia-Pacific region integrated anti-corruption solutions in service delivery.
2. Two (2) countries in Asia-Pacific region implemented social accountability initiatives. 
3. Six (6) countries have been trained or received policy and advisory support to integrate anti-corruption into national development strategies and programmes.
4. Two hundred ninety six (296) practitioners from governments, civil society, academia, private sector and donor partners are trained through the online course on integrating AC in the 2030 Agenda
5. Four (4) anti-corruption institutions have strengthened their capacities, systems and measures to prevent corruption.
6. At least ten (10) additional countries have been supported across regions to strengthen anti-corruption efforts.  
7. Subscription to the anti-corruption portal increased by 9% 
8. Two (2) countries in Asia-Pacific selected to implement pilot country level projects on strengthening national capacities to integrate anti-corruption in the SDGs.
9. Three (3) global webinars were organized to exchange knowledge on integrating anti-corruption in COVID-19 response and recovery priorities; 1 regional dialogue was organized to promote South-South/Triangular Cooperation between anti-corruption agencies in Asia; and 3 global workshops were organized at the 19th International Anti-Corruption Conference.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2022-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.At least 5 digital solutions developed to strengthen national anti-corruption capacities and promote transparency, accountability, and anti-corruption in Africa and Asia &#x2013; Pacific regions.
2. At least 300 people (disaggregated by gender) reached through various webinars, workshops, and capacity building activities.
3.Bangladesh, DRC, Liberia, Malawi, Nepal, Nigeria, Rwanda, Sri Lanka, Tanzania, Uganda, and Vietnam further strengthen their capacity to deliver Anti-corruption Innovation Initiative.
4.At least 10,000 people reached through public awareness events and campaigns.
5.At least two knowledge products are developed to contribute to global knowledge on emerging anti-corruption issues.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">Result for target 1: Eight (8) digital solutions in Nepal, Bangladesh, Tanzania, Liberia, Uganda, Malawi, Rwanda, and DRC were developed and launched to strengthen national capacities to promote transparency, accountability, and anti-corruption. 
Results for target 2: Two thousand two hundred sixty (2,260) people (939 female and 1,321 male) reached through various webinars, workshops and capacity-building activities.
Result for target 3: Bangladesh, DRC, Liberia, Malawi, Nepal, Nigeria, Rwanda, Sri Lanka, Tanzania, Uganda, and Vietnam have significantly enhanced their capacities to deliver meaningful and agile anti-corruption reforms through Anti-corruption Innovation Initiative.
Result for target 4. Over three million (3,000,000) people were reached through public events and campaigns aimed at harnessing the knowledge and boosting citizens&#x2019; awareness on corruption prevention mechanisms 
Result for target 5: Three (3) knowledge products were developed to contribute to global knowledge on emerging anti-corruption issues. These include the &#x2018;Ant-corruption Toolkit&#x2019;, &#x2018;The Online Course on Public Procurement&#x2019;, and &#x2018;The UNDP - UNSSC Course on Anti-corruption in the Context of 2030 agenda&#x2019;.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
